      "id": 1,
      "text": "Whitesville is a former coal town in Boone County located along the Big Coal River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitesville%2C_West_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Whitesville was incorporated on August 15, 1935, and named for an early settler named B. White.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4,
        12,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitesville%2C_West_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Before becoming Whitesville, this community was known as Jarrold's Valley and Pritchard City.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitesville%2C_West_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The arrival of the Norfolk and Western and Chesapeake and Ohio railroads in the 1890s catalyzed a boom in coal extraction.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/whitesville_west_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Whitesville occupies a narrow stretch of land between the confluences of Toms Branch and Seng Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitesville%2C_West_Virginia"
    },
    {
